

📘 DFDL Data Modeling – Short Notes

🔹 Meaning:

DFDL schema = data ka blueprint (structure + format).

Help karta hai raw data (CSV, fixed-length, binary, etc.) ko understandable model me convert karne me.



---

🔹 Analogy with Programming:

Programming	DFDL

Learn theory (syntax, OOP)	Learn DFDL theory (annotations, lengthKind, encoding)
Use IDE like Eclipse	Use DFDL editor in IBM Toolkit
Convert real problem into code	Convert raw data into DFDL structure



---

🔹 When Metadata is Available:

Example: COBOL copybook / C struct.

IBM ke paas importer tools hote hai → directly DFDL schema ban jata hai (no manual work).



---

🔹 When Metadata is NOT Available:

You only have raw data sample (e.g. CSV).

Analyze manually:

Separator = ,

Fields = ID, Name, Age

Record repeats per line


Create DFDL schema manually with:

dfdl:separator=","

dfdl:terminator="%NL;"

dfdl:lengthKind="delimited"




---

🔹 Stages of Data Modeling:

1. Understand Logical Structure → e.g. Record → (ID, Name, Age)


2. Configure DFDL Annotations → separators, datatypes, encoding, etc.


3. Organize Schema → use sequence, element, choice properly.




---

🔹 Example – CSV DFDL Schema

<xs:sequence dfdl:separator="%NL;">
  <xs:element name="Record" maxOccurs="unbounded">
    <xs:sequence dfdl:separator=",">
      <xs:element name="ID" type="xs:int"/>
      <xs:element name="Name" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="Age" type="xs:int"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:element>
</xs:sequence>


---

🔹 Bottom Line:

Theory + Tool seekhna easy.

Real skill: raw data ko dekhkar uska structure samajhna aur schema likhna.

Metadata mile → import tool use kar.

Metadata na mile → manual analysis + DFDL schema likh

DFDL = programming jaisa sochna → pehle logic samajh, fir annotations likh, fir test kar. ✅

